// TEST: Struct Negative Literal Field
// ============================================================================
// REGRESSION PROBE, and deliberately the MINIMAL one: a plain struct — no
// bounds, no where-clause, nothing to do with index types — constructed with a
// NEGATIVE integer literal in a field.
//
// This failed at HEAD. `P { a = 1 }` succeeded while `P { a = -1 }` was
// refused with "expected Int32, got Int64": a bare int literal was retyped to
// the expected scalar during checking, but a NEGATED literal fell through to
// inference instead, which types it Int64. The negation was the whole
// difference.
//
// It surfaced through the constrained-record work because every m-value family
// in that domain is symmetric about zero — m ∈ [-l, l] — so essentially every
// anchor example hits it. But the bug was never about bounds or about static
// structs, and a probe written in terms of either would document it wrongly
// and would stop protecting it the moment those features moved. Hence this
// file: the smallest program that would have caught it, in the category where
// a reader looking for struct-construction behavior will actually find it.
//
// Both signs and both float/int are covered, since the fix is per-literal-kind
// and a one-sided probe would not notice half of it regressing.
// ============================================================================
struct P {
    a: Int,
    b: Int,
    x: Float
}

let neg = P { a = -1, b = 2, x = -0.5 }
let a = neg.a
// EXPECT: a = -1
let b = neg.b
// EXPECT: b = 2
let x = neg.x
// EXPECT: x = -0.5

// The positive control — the case that always worked, so a failure here
// separates "the fix regressed" from "the fix broke something else".
let pos = P { a = 1, b = -2, x = 0.5 }
let c = pos.a
// EXPECT: c = 1
let d = pos.b
// EXPECT: d = -2
